"[2511.01884v1] CudaForge: An Agent Framework with Hardware Feedback for CUDA Kernel Optimization (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.01884v1)\nciteturn2311view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.01884v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 169\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 23 Oct 2025 (this version), latest version 5 Nov 2025 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:CudaForge: An Agent Framework with Hardware Feedback for CUDA Kernel Optimization\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Zijian Zhang , cite7†Rong Wang , cite8†Shiyang Li , cite9†Yuebo Luo , cite10†Mingyi Hong , cite11†Caiwen Ding L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled CudaForge: An Agent Framework with Hardware Feedback for CUDA Kernel Optimization, by Zijian Zhang and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Developing efficient CUDA kernels is increasingly critical for AI applications such as large-scale LLM training. However, manual kernel design is both costly and time-consuming, motivating automatic approaches that leverage LLMs for code generation. Existing methods for automatic kernel generation, however, often produce low-efficiency kernels, incur high computational overhead, and fail to generalize across settings.\nL17: In this work, we propose CudaForge, a training-free multi-agent workflow for CUDA kernel generation and optimization. Our workflow is inspired by the iterative workflow of human experts, which contains steps such as developing initial kernels, testing correctness, analyzing hardware feedback, and iterative improvement.\nL18: More specifically, CudaForge employs two LLM agents: a Coder and a Judge, that iteratively generate, correct, and optimize CUDA kernels, while integrating hardware feedback such as Nsight Compute (NCU) metrics. In extensive evaluations, we show that CudaForge, by leveraging base models like OpenAI-o3, achieves 97.6\\% correctness of generated kernels and an average 1.68$\\times$ speedup over PyTorch baselines, substantially surpassing state-of-the-art models including OpenAI-o3 and Kevin on KernelBench.\nL19: Beyond accuracy and speed, CudaForge demonstrates strong generalization across GPUs (A100, RTX 6000, 4090, 3090) and base models (OpenAI-o3, GPT-5, gpt-oss-120B, Claude-Sonnet-4, QwQ-32B), while maintaining high efficiency. In particular, generating an optimized kernel takes about 26.5 minutes on one RTX6000 and incurs about \\$ 0.3 API cost, which is significantly cheaper than existing agentic work that costs 6 H100 hours and \\$ 5 API cost per kernel.\nL20: Our results highlight that multi-agent, training-free workflows can enable cost-effective, generalizable, and high-performance CUDA kernel optimization. Code available at cite14†this https URL†github.com L21: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite15†arXiv:2511.01884 [cs.LG]\nL23:    | (or cite16†arXiv:2511.01884v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL24:    | cite17†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.01884†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Zijian Zhang [cite18†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Thu, 23 Oct 2025 22:52:00 UTC (1,351 KB)\nL29: cite5†[v2] Wed, 5 Nov 2025 02:10:35 UTC (1,348 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled CudaForge: An Agent Framework with Hardware Feedback for CUDA Kernel Optimization, by Zijian Zhang and 5 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite12†View PDF L38:   * cite13†HTML (exp